{
  "pedal": "Boss DD-8 Digital Delay — the loop: what limits it, what it does at high Feedback, mode by mode (mono: Output A)",
  "comment": "Follows the focused reamp of 2026-11-09 and what its listening pairs showed (the clip on each repeat as it leaves the line, a limit on the way in, soft in Analog; 17 samples more per pass). Three blocks, Level and Time at 12:00 throughout. Feedback 12:00: per mode a ladder of 50 ms bursts at 1 kHz and at 300 Hz from -18 to +3 dB, each followed by its repeats in silence (the first repeat against the level is the clip, the second against the first is the line's limit and its knee; at 300 Hz the clip's harmonics pass Analog's and Tape's filters), the stabs at -12 dB in Analog, Tape and Warm (under the clip), Warm's sweep with Feedback (where its tilt sits), Tape's and Mod's stabs with a probe tone under the guitar (its phase on the repeats is the wow and the sweep, moment by moment). Feedback 3:00, the knob set once and left: the stabs in all five modes (each mode's fade against Standard's on the same knob; many passes: the loop's saturation) and at -12 dB in Standard, Analog and Tape (the loop alone, nothing clipping). Feedback 5:00: one burst over the clip in Standard, Analog and Tape, 16 s (what holds the loop). The marker goes through the pedal on the guitar takes only (its repeats are a probe there); on ladders, sweeps and bursts it would land on the signal. OUTPUT A to input 2, nothing in OUTPUT B; the pedal on its adapter; the CARRYOVER switch as it was (on). Clock positions: 7:00 = fully counter-clockwise, 5:00 = fully clockwise; 9:00 and 3:00 pointer horizontal.",
  "quiet_dbfs": -60,
  "knobs": [
    "Mode",
    "Level",
    "Feedback",
    "Time"
  ],
